#!/bin/bash
# Configure macOS desktop environment.
#
# Sets up:
# - Dvorak keyboard layout
# - Rotate Control, Command and Option (for Linux-like Ctrl shortcuts)
# - Spotlight and Switch to Desktop 1-9 on Option key (physical Win/Super), and
#   Move left/right a space on Control+Option+arrow, which the conf repo's
#   Karabiner rule puts under physical Win+Left/Right
# - Disables auto-correct, auto-capitalize, smart quotes/dashes
# - tide, the tiling window manager by default: a Hammerspoon config with
#   tide's tiling and named workspaces of its own, done by minimizing windows
#   rather than with Spaces. It clones tide, or pulls an existing checkout
#   (--ff-only), and installs it. It needs the keys the Amethyst setup binds,
#   so it hands Spotlight back Cmd+Space, turns the Switch to Desktop and
#   move-a-Space hotkeys off, and turns off the Stage Manager and Sequoia
#   tiling features that would fight it. It also keeps the menu bar shown,
#   for tide's own bar to cover, and installs blueutil (Homebrew) so that bar can show
#   whether Bluetooth is on, and m1ddc (Apple silicon) or ddcctl (Intel) so it
#   can show and set a monitor's speaker volume over DDC. See configure_tide and tide's SPEC.md (sections 6.4,
#   7.3 and 8), which say what it needs from here.
# - Karabiner-Elements, for the bindings the modifier rotation can't carry:
#   Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Left/Right word movement, Win+Left/Right Space switching,
#   Win+Shift+Left/Right moving the window a Space (with Amethyst), and Ctrl
#   as Control in kitty. Its rules are symlinked out of the conf repo too.
# - Or, with --wm amethyst, the Amethyst tiling window manager instead:
#   installs it (Homebrew, or a staged bundle offline). Its layouts, modifiers
#   and margins come from ~/.amethyst.yml, which confinst symlinks out of the
#   conf repo. Focus follows mouse is turned off -- see configure_amethyst for
#   why it can't carry over from KDE.
# - LinearMouse, so a mouse wheel notch scrolls nine lines rather than one, and
#   the pointer accelerates more gently than macOS's curve: its config is
#   written only where there's none, or only the empty one it makes
#   as it first starts, since the app rewrites it.
# - Application launch shortcuts via Automator Quick Actions (browser, terminal,
#   music)
# - Finder preferences (show hidden files, path bar, file extensions)
# - Screenshots saved to ~/Pictures/Screenshots, tide's folder, without the
#   floating thumbnail that holds each back from it for a few seconds
#
# Modifier key philosophy (matches Linux muscle memory):
#   Physical Ctrl (pinky)    → Command  → copy, paste, tabs, close
#   Physical Win/Super       → Option   → launcher, desktop switching
#   Physical Alt (by space)  → Control  → available for per-app custom shortcuts
#
# The modifier remap is a three-key rotation, not a swap, and hidutil applies
# it to every attached keyboard -- including a laptop's built-in one, whose
# keys are already in the Mac order this rotation is undoing. It assumes a PC
# keyboard.
#
# Not everything here reaches the running session. The `defaults` writes go to
# preference domains their owners read at launch (Finder and the Dock are
# restarted below; other apps pick the text-substitution settings up as they
# start), and the input source is read by the login session, so the layout
# change needs a logout. Each step says which of those applies, and the run
# ends by saying whether anything didn't work.
#
# Requires: defaults, hidutil (i.e. macOS). Both are checked up front, so
#           running this anywhere else says so instead of failing several
#           steps in with "defaults: not found".
# Wants: brew, to install Amethyst (a bundle staged at $AMETHYST_BUNDLE,
#        ./amethyst.zip or ~/amethyst.zip is the offline path, and unzip and
#        xattr unpack it); activateSettings, to apply the shortcut changes
#        without a logout;
#        launchctl, to load the key-remapping agent now rather than at the
#        next login; pbs, to register the Quick Actions; killall and pgrep, to
#        restart Finder and the Dock. Without any of them the settings are
#        still written and the script says what needs a logout.
#
# docs/MACOS.md covers the whole setup: the key mapping against the KDE/Krohnkite
# side, how the symbolic hotkeys work, what doesn't carry over from KWin, and
# why Amethyst rather than yabai or AeroSpace.
#
# Two things here need a human first -- creating the Spaces and quitting System
# Settings -- and a third, granting Amethyst Accessibility access, needs one
# afterwards, since a first run installs Amethyst partway through and there is
# nothing to grant the permission to until it has. None of the three can be
# done from a script. The run opens by saying so and waiting for Enter; see
# preflight. --yes skips the wait, and so does a non-terminal stdin, so an
# unattended bootstrap doesn't block on an Enter that can never arrive.
#
# Usage:
#     setup-macos [--no-install] [--no-sudo] [--ignore-errors] [--yes]
#                 [--wm tide|amethyst] [-v | --verbose] [-h | --help]
#
# setup forwards --no-install, --no-sudo, --ignore-errors and --verbose here.
# --no-install skips installing tide (or Amethyst) and Karabiner-Elements and
# configures whatever is already there. --no-sudo skips the two steps here that
# need administrator rights: Karabiner's install -- its cask is a .pkg and
# Homebrew hands those to the system installer -- and the hostname on the lock
# screen. --ignore-errors keeps going past
# failures instead of aborting on the first one. --yes skips the preflight
# pause. --wm picks the tiling window manager: tide (or timo, its old name),
# the default, so a plain setup sets it up, or amethyst. setup doesn't forward
# --wm, so an Amethyst machine runs this a second time with --wm amethyst.

# Whether to keep going past failures. "no" aborts on the first error via
# set -e; "yes" (via --ignore-errors) leaves it off and pushes through.
IGNORE_ERRORS=no

# Whether to show what Homebrew prints (--verbose), rather than keep it in the
# log.
VERBOSE=no

# Whether to install Amethyst. --no-install skips it and configures whatever is
# already there, matching setup-kde, which installs its own tiling window
# manager (Krohnkite) under the same flag.
INSTALL=yes

# Which tiling window manager to set up (--wm): tide, the default, or
# amethyst. A plain `setup`, which passes no --wm, sets up tide.
WM=tide

# Where tide's checkout lives, or will once cloned. A checkout rather than a
# package because tide is a Hammerspoon config with no release to install:
# `make install` copies its Lua out of the checkout into ~/.hammerspoon.
#
# tide's repo is tidemo. It was tilemo, and timo before that, and earlier
# runs cloned it into ~/tilemo or ~/timo (or took $TILEMO_DIR or $TIMO_DIR).
# Each is still honored, so a machine set up then reuses its checkout rather
# than cloning a second copy beside it; see install_tide.
TIDEMO_DIR="${TIDEMO_DIR:-${TILEMO_DIR:-${TIMO_DIR:-}}}"
if test -z "$TIDEMO_DIR"; then
    TIDEMO_DIR="$HOME/tidemo"
    for _old in "$HOME/tilemo" "$HOME/timo"; do
        if ! test -e "$TIDEMO_DIR" && test -d "$_old"; then
            TIDEMO_DIR="$_old"
        fi
    done
fi
TIDEMO_REPO="https://github.com/mikelward/tidemo.git"

# Whether to skip the preflight pause (--yes). A stdin that isn't a terminal
# skips it too; see preflight.
ASSUME_YES=no

# Whether privileged commands may run at all. Karabiner's install needs this:
# its cask is a .pkg and Homebrew hands those to the system installer, which
# prompts for an administrator password. So does the lock screen's hostname,
# which lives in a system-wide file. --no-sudo skips both.
SUDO=yes

# Homebrew's ask mode is on by default, so the cask installs below stop to
# confirm a plan the caller already asked for. setup exports this too; set it
# here as well because setup-macos is also run on its own. It stops at
# confirmations -- the administrator password Karabiner's .pkg needs is the
# user's to give, and nothing here suppresses it.
export HOMEBREW_NO_ASK=1
# And, as in setup, a brew install leaves installed casks and formulae at
# their versions: setup upgrades them at most once a week.
export HOMEBREW_NO_INSTALL_UPGRADE=1
export HOMEBREW_NO_INSTALLED_DEPENDENTS_CHECK=1
export HOMEBREW_AUTO_UPDATE_SECS=604800

# Directory containing this script, resolved up front while $0 still means what
# the caller typed. The launcher helpers the Quick Actions run live beside this
# script, and during a fresh bootstrap `setup` runs it straight out of the
# just-cloned repo, before any login shell has put it on PATH.
SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"

# macOS helpers that live at an absolute path rather than on PATH, so PATH
# can't shadow them. Overridable so the tests can point them at mocks, in the
# same spirit as setup-kde's $PYTHON.
#
# activateSettings makes the symbolic hotkey writes take effect without a
# logout; pbs re-reads the Services registry so the Quick Actions and their key
# equivalents are picked up.
ACTIVATE_SETTINGS="${ACTIVATE_SETTINGS:-/System/Library/PrivateFrameworks/SystemAdministration.framework/Resources/activateSettings}"
PBS="${PBS:-/System/Library/CoreServices/pbs}"

# Where applications live. Overridable for the same reason as the helpers
# above: the tests can't write to the real /Applications.
APPLICATIONS_DIR="${APPLICATIONS_DIR:-/Applications}"
USER_APPLICATIONS_DIR="${USER_APPLICATIONS_DIR:-$HOME/Applications}"

# Count of steps that warned, so the closing message can say the run was
# incomplete instead of claiming success over a screenful of warnings. Only
# reachable with --ignore-errors: without it set -e aborts on the first
# failure.
WARNINGS=0

# Dvorak as an input source entry, used both to enable the layout and to
# select it. The layout ID is the part that identifies it: macOS ships several
# variants whose names all contain "Dvorak" (Dvorak - Qwerty <Cmd>, Dvorak
# Left, Dvorak Right), so matching the name would confuse standard Dvorak with
# any of them.
DVORAK_LAYOUT_ID=16300
DVORAK_INPUT_SOURCE="<dict><key>InputSourceKind</key><string>Keyboard Layout</string><key>KeyboardLayout ID</key><integer>$DVORAK_LAYOUT_ID</integer><key>KeyboardLayout Name</key><string>Dvorak</string></dict>"

# Every warning and error goes to $SETUP_PROBLEMS too, so the run ends by
# repeating them all: one line among thousands scrolls by unseen. The
# outermost of setup and its helpers (setup-kde, setup-tide, setup-macos)
# makes the file and repeats it as it exits, however it exits; a helper it
# runs inherits the file and only adds to it.
note_problem() {
    test -n "${SETUP_PROBLEMS:-}" || return 0
    printf '%s\n' "$1" >>"$SETUP_PROBLEMS" ||
        printf "Warning: couldn't add that to %s, so the end of the run won't repeat it\n" "$SETUP_PROBLEMS" >&2
}

PROBLEMS_OWNER=no
start_problem_recap() {
    test -z "${SETUP_PROBLEMS:-}" || return 0
    if ! SETUP_PROBLEMS=$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/setup-problems-XXXXXX"); then
        SETUP_PROBLEMS=
        warn "couldn't make a file to collect warnings in, so the end of the run won't repeat them"
        return 0
    fi
    export SETUP_PROBLEMS
    PROBLEMS_OWNER=yes
}

# recap_problems STATUS, from the EXIT trap: repeats every warning and error,
# says if the run stopped early, and keeps STATUS as the exit status.
recap_problems() {
    test "$PROBLEMS_OWNER" = yes || return "$1"
    if test -s "$SETUP_PROBLEMS"; then
        printf '\n%s: the warnings and errors from this run, again:\n' "setup-macos" >&2
        sed 's/^/  /' "$SETUP_PROBLEMS" >&2
    fi
    if test "$1" -ne 0; then
        printf '%s stopped early, with exit status %s\n' "setup-macos" "$1" >&2
    fi
    rm -f "$SETUP_PROBLEMS"
    return "$1"
}

info() {
    printf "%s\n" "$*"
}

# detail: an info line that only --verbose shows, for what was already so.
detail() {
    test "${VERBOSE:-no}" = yes || return 0
    info "$@"
}

warn() {
    printf "Warning: %s\n" "$*" >&2
    note_problem "Warning: $*"
    WARNINGS=$((WARNINGS + 1))
}

# What only you can do -- grant a permission, click a button in another
# app's window, log out -- collected as the run goes and listed as the last
# thing it prints, where it won't scroll away above the rest of the run.
# Each of todo's arguments is a paragraph of the step, and a line starting
# "- " in one is a bullet. todo_optional's is one paragraph. needs_logout
# names what the log-out step, always the last, is for.
TODO_STEPS=""
TODO_COUNT=0
TODO_OPTIONAL=""
LOGOUT_FOR=""

# Each line of stdin as a paragraph wrapped at 76 columns: the first after
# $1, the rest indented to match it, and a bullet's own lines under its text.
wrap_text() {
    awk -v first="$1" -v width=76 '
        function spaces(n,    s) { s = ""; while (n-- > 0) s = s " "; return s }
        BEGIN { pad = spaces(length(first)); lead = first }
        {
            text = $0
            if (text ~ /^- /) { start = lead "- "; hang = pad "  "; text = substr(text, 3) }
            else { start = lead; hang = pad }
            line = start
            words = 0
            n = split(text, w, " ")
            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
                if (words > 0 && length(line) + 1 + length(w[i]) > width) {
                    print line
                    line = hang w[i]
                } else {
                    line = (words > 0 ? line " " : line) w[i]
                }
                words++
            }
            print line
            lead = pad
        }'
}

todo() {
    TODO_COUNT=$((TODO_COUNT + 1))
    _todo_lead="  $TODO_COUNT. "
    for _todo_part in "$@"; do
        TODO_STEPS="$TODO_STEPS
$(printf '%s\n' "$_todo_part" | wrap_text "$_todo_lead")"
        _todo_lead="     "
    done
    TODO_STEPS="$TODO_STEPS
"
}

todo_optional() {
    TODO_OPTIONAL="$TODO_OPTIONAL
$(printf '%s\n' "$1" | wrap_text "  - ")"
}

needs_logout() {
    LOGOUT_FOR="${LOGOUT_FOR:+$LOGOUT_FOR, }$1"
}

# The Accessibility step for app $1, with $2 saying what it's for, then any
# more lines. No defaults key grants it: macOS asks a person.
todo_accessibility() {
    _ax_app="$1"
    _ax_why="$2"
    shift 2
    todo "Turn on Accessibility for $_ax_app. $_ax_why" \
        "In System Settings > Privacy & Security > Accessibility, turn on $_ax_app. If it isn't in the list, click + under the list, choose $_ax_app in Applications, and turn it on." \
        "$@"
}

print_todo() {
    if test -n "$LOGOUT_FOR"; then
        # "a, b, c" as "a, b and c".
        _logout_for=$(printf '%s' "$LOGOUT_FOR" | sed 's/\(.*\), /\1 and /')
        todo "Log out and back in, once the steps above are done, for $_logout_for to take effect: Apple menu > Log Out."
    fi
    if test "$TODO_COUNT" -eq 0 && test -z "$TODO_OPTIONAL"; then
        return 0
    fi
    info ""
    info "Still to do by hand -- this script can't:"
    printf '%s' "$TODO_STEPS"
    if test -n "$TODO_OPTIONAL"; then
        info ""
        info "And if you like:"
        printf '%s\n' "$TODO_OPTIONAL"
    fi
}

error() {
    printf "Error: %s\n" "$*" >&2
    note_problem "Error: $*"
    exit 1
}

is_command() {
    command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# Run a configuration step, and make a failure visible either way.
#
# Without --ignore-errors, set -e would abort on a bare failing command, and
# this aborts with the command named instead of silently. With it, set -e is
# off -- so a bare failing command's status went nowhere at all: the run
# carried on, WARNINGS stayed at zero, and the closing summary reported
# success over a step that hadn't happened. That's the whole point of the
# summary, so every state-changing step goes through here.
try() {
    "$@" && return 0
    report_failure $? "$@"
}

# What a failed step does, split out of try so a step that can't be run
# through try -- sourcing the local hook -- reports identically rather than
# growing its own wording.
report_failure() {
    _status="$1"
    shift
    if test "$IGNORE_ERRORS" = yes; then
        warn "failed (exit $_status): $*"
        return 0
    fi
    error "failed (exit $_status): $*"
}

# Write stdin to FILE, as a command whose failure `try` can actually see.
#
# `try cat > "$file"` doesn't do that: the shell opens the target before it
# invokes try, so a target that can't be opened -- a read-only file, a missing
# directory -- skips try altogether and the failure goes unrecorded. Doing the
# redirection in here puts it inside the command whose status try checks.
#
# Written to a temporary file and moved into place, so a failure part-way
# through leaves the previous file rather than a truncated plist for launchd
# or Automator to choke on.
write_file() {
    _dest="$1"
    _tmp="$_dest.tmp.$$"
    # `mv file dir` moves the file *into* dir rather than failing, so without
    # this the plist would be quietly filed somewhere else and reported
    # written.
    if test -d "$_dest"; then
        printf "%s is a directory, not a file\n" "$_dest" >&2
        return 1
    fi
    if ! cat > "$_tmp"; then
        rm -f "$_tmp"
        return 1
    fi
    if ! mv "$_tmp" "$_dest"; then
        rm -f "$_tmp"
        return 1
    fi
}

# Whether APP is running, as pgrep's own exit status: 0 running, 1 not
# running, anything else a pgrep failure.
#
# pgrep exits 1 for "no such process", but 2 and 3 for its own failures.
# Reading every non-zero status as "not running" would skip a restart on a
# broken probe and say nothing about why, so the failure statuses are passed
# through rather than collapsed into "no" and each caller decides what an
# unknown answer means.
app_running() {
    pgrep -x "$1" >/dev/null
}

# Restart APP so it re-reads the settings just written, where WHY names what
# stays unapplied if it can't be. APP not running is the ordinary case on a
# fresh machine and isn't a failure -- but it's not the only reason killall
# exits non-zero, so ask pgrep first rather than reading every non-zero status
# as "it wasn't running" and reporting a refused kill as success.
restart_app() {
    _app="$1"
    _why="$2"
    if ! is_command killall || ! is_command pgrep; then
        warn "killall/pgrep not found; restart $_app (or log out) for $_why"
        return 0
    fi
    if app_running "$_app"; then
        _running=0
    else
        _running=$?
    fi
    case "$_running" in
        0) ;;
        1)
            info "$_app was not running; it will pick up the new settings when it starts"
            return 0
            ;;
        *)
            warn "could not tell whether $_app is running (pgrep exited $_running); log out for $_why"
            return 0
            ;;
    esac
    killall "$_app" || warn "could not restart $_app; log out for $_why"
}

# --- Preflight ---

# What has to be done by hand before the run changes anything, printed
# first so it isn't found later as a dead keybinding: no API creates a
# Space, and System Settings, left open, writes its cached shortcuts back
# over these. The rest waits for the end-of-run list, since most of it is
# for apps the run installs. tide uses one Space per display, so the
# nine-Spaces step is Amethyst's alone.
preflight_notes() {
    info ""
    info "Before continuing:"
    if test "$WM" = amethyst; then
        info "  1. Create nine Spaces: open Mission Control and click + until there"
        info "     are nine."
        info "  2. Quit System Settings, or it can undo the shortcuts set here."
    else
        info "  1. Quit System Settings, or it can undo the shortcuts set here."
    fi
    info "The rest is listed at the end of the run."
    info ""
}

preflight() {
    preflight_notes
    if test "$ASSUME_YES" = yes; then
        return 0
    fi
    # Nothing to prompt on: setup runs this as part of an unattended bootstrap,
    # which must not block on an Enter that can never arrive.
    if ! test -t 0; then
        info "stdin is not a terminal; continuing without pausing."
        return 0
    fi
    printf "Press Enter to continue, or Ctrl-C to abort: "
    # EOF rather than a line means stdin closed under us; treat it as consent
    # for the same reason as the non-terminal case, and end the line the
    # prompt left open so the first step doesn't print onto it.
    if ! read -r _preflight_reply; then
        info ""
    fi
}

usage() {
    cat <<'USAGE'
Usage: setup-macos [--no-install] [--no-sudo] [--ignore-errors] [--yes]
                   [--wm tide|amethyst] [-v | --verbose] [-h | --help]

Configure the macOS desktop environment.

Options:
  --no-install     Don't install Hammerspoon, tide (or, with --wm amethyst,
                   Amethyst), Karabiner-Elements or LinearMouse; configure
                   whatever is already there
  --no-sudo        Skip what needs administrator rights (Karabiner's installer)
  --ignore-errors  Keep going past failures instead of aborting on the first
  --yes            Don't pause before starting
  --wm WM          The tiling window manager: tide (the default) or amethyst
  -v, --verbose    Show what Homebrew prints, instead of keeping it in
                   ~/.local/state/setup/setup.log, and what was already so
  -h, --help       Show this help and exit
USAGE
}

# --- Parse arguments ---

while test $# -gt 0; do
    case "$1" in
        --install)
            INSTALL=yes
            ;;
        --no-install)
            INSTALL=no
            ;;
        --sudo)
            SUDO=yes
            ;;
        --no-sudo)
            # Almost nothing here needs sudo -- installing Amethyst needs write
            # access to an Applications directory rather than privilege, and a
            # staged bundle falls back to ~/Applications when /Applications
            # isn't writable. Karabiner is the exception: its cask is backed by
            # a .pkg, so Homebrew hands it to the system installer, which asks
            # for an administrator password. setup promises --no-sudo never
            # prompts or stalls, so that install has to be skipped rather than
            # attempted. See install_karabiner.
            SUDO=no
            ;;
        --ignore-errors)
            IGNORE_ERRORS=yes
            ;;
        -v|--verbose)
            VERBOSE=yes
            ;;
        -y|--yes)
            ASSUME_YES=yes
            ;;
        --wm)
            if test $# -lt 2; then
                warn "--wm needs a value: amethyst or tide"
                usage >&2
                exit 1
            fi
            shift
            WM="$1"
            ;;
        --wm=*)
            WM="${1#--wm=}"
            ;;
        -h|--help)
            usage
            exit 0
            ;;
        *)
            warn "Unknown option: $1"
            usage >&2
            exit 1
            ;;
    esac
    shift
done

case "$WM" in
    amethyst|tide) ;;
    # tide's names before its repo was renamed, so a command line or note
    # from then still works.
    tilemo|timo)
        WM=tide
        ;;
    *)
        warn "Unknown window manager for --wm: '$WM' (expected amethyst or tide)"
        usage >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
esac

# From here on, the run ends by repeating its warnings and errors.
start_problem_recap
trap 'recap_problems "$?"' EXIT

# Abort on the first error by default. --ignore-errors leaves set -e off so
# the script pushes through failing steps.
if test "$IGNORE_ERRORS" = no; then
    set -e
fi

# --- Check prerequisites ---

# Every step below is a `defaults` write or a hidutil remap, so without those
# two there is nothing this script can do. Checked up front, and by name, so
# running it on the wrong OS says so rather than failing several steps in.
# The rest of the macOS helpers are checked where they're used: each has a
# documented fallback (a logout, mostly).
is_command defaults || error "defaults is required (this script configures macOS)"
is_command hidutil || error "hidutil is required (this script configures macOS)"

# --- Configure keyboard ---

configure_keyboard() {
    info "Configuring keyboard"

    # Set Dvorak as the input source. AppleEnabledInputSources is the list of
    # available layouts and AppleSelectedInputSources the active one, so both
    # are needed for Dvorak to be picked rather than merely offered.
    #
    # -array-add appends unconditionally, so re-running setup used to stack a
    # second, third, fourth Dvorak entry in the list. Add it only when it isn't
    # there already. A `defaults read` of a key that was never written prints a
    # "does not exist" diagnostic and exits non-zero; here that is the answer
    # "no layouts enabled yet", not a failure to report.
    #
    # Matched on the layout ID, not the name: "Dvorak - Qwerty <Cmd>" contains
    # "Dvorak" too, so a machine with only that variant enabled would look
    # like it already had standard Dvorak -- and the select below would then
    # point at a source missing from the enabled list.
    if defaults read com.apple.HIToolbox AppleEnabledInputSources 2>/dev/null \
            | grep -qE "\"?KeyboardLayout ID\"? *= *$DVORAK_LAYOUT_ID *;"; then
        detail "Skipping Dvorak: already an enabled input source"
    else
        try defaults write com.apple.HIToolbox AppleEnabledInputSources -array-add \
            "$DVORAK_INPUT_SOURCE"
    fi
    # -array, not -array-add: this is the single selected source, so replacing
    # the value outright is both correct and idempotent.
    try defaults write com.apple.HIToolbox AppleSelectedInputSources -array \
        "$DVORAK_INPUT_SOURCE"
    try defaults write com.apple.HIToolbox AppleCurrentKeyboardLayoutInputSourceID \
        "com.apple.keylayout.Dvorak"
    # The login session reads the input source at startup and holds it in
    # memory, so unlike the hidutil remap below this one isn't live yet.
    info "The Dvorak layout takes effect at the next login"
    needs_logout "the Dvorak layout"
    todo_optional "Remove the keyboard layouts you don't want: System Settings > Keyboard, then Edit beside Input Sources, then select one and click -."

    # Disable auto-correct, auto-capitalize, smart quotes/dashes. Read by each
    # app as it launches, so already-running apps keep the old behavior. With
    # tide, its macOS settings do this (write_tide_macos_settings).
    test "$WM" = tide && return 0
    try defaults write NSGlobalDomain NSAutomaticSpellingCorrectionEnabled -bool false
    try defaults write NSGlobalDomain NSAutomaticCapitalizationEnabled -bool false
    try defaults write NSGlobalDomain NSAutomaticQuoteSubstitutionEnabled -bool false
    try defaults write NSGlobalDomain NSAutomaticDashSubstitutionEnabled -bool false
    try defaults write NSGlobalDomain NSAutomaticPeriodSubstitutionEnabled -bool false

}

# --- Remap modifier keys ---

# The remap, as one line, so the live hidutil call and the LaunchAgent that
# reapplies it at login can't drift apart. They used to be two hand-maintained
# copies of the same JSON.
#
# Remap modifier keys to match Linux muscle memory:
#   Physical Ctrl (E0/E4) → Command (E3/E7)
#   Physical Win  (E3/E7) → Option  (E2/E6)
#   Physical Alt  (E2/E6) → Control (E0/E4)
KEY_ROTATION='{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x7000000E0,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x7000000E3},{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x7000000E3,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x7000000E2},{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x7000000E2,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x7000000E0},{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x7000000E4,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x7000000E7},{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x7000000E7,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x7000000E6},{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x7000000E6,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x7000000E4}'
# With tide, Caps Lock (39) sends F18 (6D) too, which tide takes as
# Compose, as Linux's Caps Lock is (tide SPEC.md §10). Amethyst has no use
# for F18, so there Caps Lock stays.
KEY_CAPS_COMPOSE='{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x700000039,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x70000006D}'
if test "$WM" = tide; then
    KEY_REMAPPING="{\"UserKeyMapping\":[$KEY_ROTATION,$KEY_CAPS_COMPOSE]}"
else
    KEY_REMAPPING="{\"UserKeyMapping\":[$KEY_ROTATION]}"
fi

configure_modifier_keys() {
    if test "$WM" = tide; then
        info "Remapping modifier keys (Ctrl→Cmd, Win→Option, Alt→Control) and Caps Lock to Compose"
    else
        info "Remapping modifier keys (Ctrl→Cmd, Win→Option, Alt→Control)"
    fi

    # Applies to the running session immediately; hidutil mappings are lost on
    # reboot, which is what the LaunchAgent below is for.
    try hidutil property --set "$KEY_REMAPPING" >/dev/null

    # Persist across reboots with a LaunchAgent
    launch_agent_dir="$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents"
    launch_agent="$launch_agent_dir/com.local.KeyRemapping.plist"
    try mkdir -p "$launch_agent_dir"

    try write_file "$launch_agent" <<PLIST
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.local.KeyRemapping</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/bin/hidutil</string>
        <string>property</string>
        <string>--set</string>
        <string>$KEY_REMAPPING</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
PLIST

    # Load it now rather than leaving it for the next login: a malformed plist
    # is worth hearing about while the script that wrote it is still running,
    # not the next time the machine boots without a working Ctrl key.
    if ! is_command launchctl; then
        warn "launchctl not found; the key remapping is live now but was not verified for the next login"
        return 0
    fi
    # A previous run's copy has to go first -- bootstrap refuses a label that's
    # already loaded -- and on the first run there is nothing to unload, which
    # is why this one failure is discarded rather than reported.
    launchctl bootout "gui/$(id -u)/com.local.KeyRemapping" 2>/dev/null || true
    launchctl bootstrap "gui/$(id -u)" "$launch_agent" \
        || warn "could not load $launch_agent; the remapping is live now but won't survive a reboot"
}

# --- Configure keyboard shortcuts ---

# System Settings holds the shortcut registry in memory while it's open and can
# flush its copy back over these writes when it closes, silently reverting
# them. Worth a warning rather than a refusal: it only clobbers if the Keyboard
# pane is actually visited, so a run with it open often still lands.
warn_if_settings_app_running() {
    if ! is_command pgrep; then
        warn "pgrep not found; quit System Settings before this run, or it can overwrite the shortcuts written below"
        return 0
    fi
    # Both names, because the app was renamed in macOS 13 and this script has
    # to work on either side of that.
    for _settings_app in "System Settings" "System Preferences"; do
        if app_running "$_settings_app"; then
            _settings_running=0
        else
            _settings_running=$?
        fi
        case "$_settings_running" in
            0)
                if test "$WM" = tide; then
                    warn "$_settings_app is running and can overwrite these shortcuts when it closes; quit it and re-run if tide's keys don't register"
                else
                    warn "$_settings_app is running and can overwrite these shortcuts when it closes; quit it and re-run if Option+1..9 doesn't work"
                fi
                ;;
            1) ;;
            *)
                warn "could not tell whether $_settings_app is running (pgrep exited $_settings_running); quit it before this run if it's open"
                ;;
        esac
    done
}

# Write one symbolic hotkey: ID, enabled (true or false), then its three
# parameters (ASCII code, virtual keycode, modifier flags). Always the whole
# entry, for the reason given at 65 below: -dict-add replaces it outright, so
# a disabled hotkey written without its parameters loses its binding.
write_symbolic_hotkey() {
    try defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add "$1" '
        <dict>
            <key>enabled</key><'"$2"'/>
            <key>value</key><dict>
                <key>parameters</key><array>
                    <integer>'"$3"'</integer>
                    <integer>'"$4"'</integer>
                    <integer>'"$5"'</integer>
                </array>
                <key>type</key><string>standard</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>'
}

# tide registers its keys with RegisterEventHotKey, which fails silently on a
# key someone else already holds (tide's SPEC.md, section 7.3), so the system
# hotkeys the Amethyst setup moved onto Option -- and the move-a-Space chord --
# have to be let go first. Each goes back to its stock binding rather than
# merely off, so the shortcut still reads sensibly in System Settings and
# turning it back on there gets the macOS default, not a blank.
configure_shortcuts_tide() {
    # Spotlight back on Cmd+Space (1048576 is Command), which is physical
    # Ctrl+Space under the rotation. That leaves Option+Space free: tide's
    # Super is Ctrl+Option+Command with the Karabiner tide rule, but falls back
    # to Option without it.
    write_symbolic_hotkey 64 true 32 49 1048576

    # Finder search back on, at its stock Cmd+Option+Space: the Amethyst
    # setup only turned it off because Option+Space was Spotlight's, and with
    # Spotlight back on Cmd+Space nothing collides with it.
    write_symbolic_hotkey 65 true 32 49 1572864

    # Switch to Desktop 1-9 (118-126) off, at their stock Ctrl+1..9 (262144 is
    # Control): tide's workspaces are its own, on Super+1..9, and don't use
    # Spaces at all. Same non-sequential keycodes as configure_shortcuts.
    set -- \
        118 49 18 \
        119 50 19 \
        120 51 20 \
        121 52 21 \
        122 53 23 \
        123 54 22 \
        124 55 26 \
        125 56 28 \
        126 57 25
    while test $# -ge 3; do
        write_symbolic_hotkey "$1" false "$2" "$3" 262144
        shift 3
    done

    # Move left/right a Space (79 and 81) off, at their stock Ctrl+arrows
    # (8650752 is Control plus the arrow keys' function-key flag). The
    # Karabiner arrow rule sends Ctrl+Option+arrow for physical Win+arrow, and
    # tide takes that chord for itself.
    write_symbolic_hotkey 79 false 65535 123 8650752
    write_symbolic_hotkey 81 false 65535 124 8650752

    # Mission Control (32) and App Exposé (33) off, at their stock Ctrl+Up
    # and Ctrl+Down: tide parks hidden workspaces' windows on one Space,
    # so Mission Control would show every workspace at once, and tide
    # takes both keys for its own overview (tide's SPEC.md, section 6.9).
    write_symbolic_hotkey 32 false 65535 126 8650752
    write_symbolic_hotkey 33 false 65535 125 8650752
}

configure_shortcuts() {
    if test "$WM" = tide; then
        info "Configuring keyboard shortcuts (Spotlight back on Cmd+Space, desktop switching off, for tide)"
        warn_if_settings_app_running
        configure_shortcuts_tide
        activate_shortcuts
        return 0
    fi

    info "Configuring keyboard shortcuts (Mission Control and Spotlight on Option)"

    warn_if_settings_app_running

    # Remap Mission Control shortcuts from Control to Option
    # so the physical Win/Super key handles desktop switching
    #
    # Parameters format: (ASCII code, virtual keycode, modifier flags)
    # Modifier flags: Option = 524288

    # Spotlight: Option+Space (ID 64)
    try defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add 64 '
        <dict>
            <key>enabled</key><true/>
            <key>value</key><dict>
                <key>parameters</key><array>
                    <integer>32</integer>
                    <integer>49</integer>
                    <integer>524288</integer>
                </array>
                <key>type</key><string>standard</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>'

    # Disable Finder search window (ID 65) to avoid conflict.
    #
    # -dict-add replaces the whole entry, so writing a bare enabled=false would
    # drop the parameters with it and leave the shortcut showing blank in
    # System Settings, with nothing to restore it to. Its stock binding
    # (Cmd+Option+Space: 1048576 + 524288) is written back alongside the flag,
    # so turning it on again in the GUI gets the original key back.
    try defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add 65 '
        <dict>
            <key>enabled</key><false/>
            <key>value</key><dict>
                <key>parameters</key><array>
                    <integer>32</integer>
                    <integer>49</integer>
                    <integer>1572864</integer>
                </array>
                <key>type</key><string>standard</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>'

    # Switch to Desktop 1-9: Option+1..9 (IDs 118-126)
    # macOS virtual keycodes for number keys are not sequential:
    #   1=18 2=19 3=20 4=21 5=23 6=22 7=26 8=28 9=25
    set -- \
        118 49 18 \
        119 50 19 \
        120 51 20 \
        121 52 21 \
        122 53 23 \
        123 54 22 \
        124 55 26 \
        125 56 28 \
        126 57 25
    while test $# -ge 3; do
        hotkey_id=$1 ascii=$2 keycode=$3
        shift 3
        try defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add "$hotkey_id" '
            <dict>
                <key>enabled</key><true/>
                <key>value</key><dict>
                    <key>parameters</key><array>
                        <integer>'"$ascii"'</integer>
                        <integer>'"$keycode"'</integer>
                        <integer>524288</integer>
                    </array>
                    <key>type</key><string>standard</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>'
    done

    # Move left/right a space: Control+Option+Left/Right (IDs 79 and 81),
    # replacing the stock Ctrl+arrows. Not bare Option+arrow, though physical
    # Win+arrow sends that: the Karabiner arrow rule synthesizes Option+arrow
    # for Ctrl+arrow word movement, and a hotkey can't tell the two apart. The
    # rule maps physical Win+arrow onto this chord instead. Arrow keys carry the
    # function-key flag (8388608) on top of Control (262144) and Option
    # (524288), and 65535 is the "no character" ASCII slot.
    set -- \
        79 123 \
        81 124
    while test $# -ge 2; do
        hotkey_id=$1 keycode=$2
        shift 2
        try defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add "$hotkey_id" '
            <dict>
                <key>enabled</key><true/>
                <key>value</key><dict>
                    <key>parameters</key><array>
                        <integer>65535</integer>
                        <integer>'"$keycode"'</integer>
                        <integer>9175040</integer>
                    </array>
                    <key>type</key><string>standard</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>'
    done

    activate_shortcuts
}

# Make the symbolic hotkey writes take effect in this session.
activate_shortcuts() {
    if test -x "$ACTIVATE_SETTINGS"; then
        "$ACTIVATE_SETTINGS" -u \
            || warn "could not apply the shortcut settings to this session; log out and back in for the shortcut changes to take effect"
    else
        warn "$ACTIVATE_SETTINGS not found; log out and back in for the shortcut changes to take effect"
    fi
}

# --- Configure Spaces ---

# Spaces per display, printed on stdout as counts separated by spaces -- one
# per display, in the order the Dock lists them.
#
# There's no public API for this, so it comes out of the Dock's own plist:
# every Space carries a ManagedSpaceID, so counting those counts Spaces. The
# count has to be per display rather than a total, because with "Displays have
# separate Spaces" enabled the desktop numbering is per display too: two
# displays with five Spaces each total ten, and Option+6..9 is dead on both.
#
# Each count can read one high, since the monitor's current-Space marker
# carries a ManagedSpaceID of its own, and can read high again for Spaces
# macOS created for fullscreen apps. So this catches "plainly too few" rather
# than auditing the exact number -- which is its whole remit. It deliberately
# makes no "all nine are fine" claim, because that's the direction an
# over-count would get wrong.
count_spaces_per_display() {
    _spaces_plist=$(defaults read com.apple.spaces SpacesDisplayConfiguration) \
        || return 1
    printf '%s\n' "$_spaces_plist" | awk '
        # Each monitor block is introduced by its display identifier, so that
        # line closes the previous block and opens the next.
        /Display Identifier/ {
            if (seen) { counts = counts " " n }
            seen = 1; n = 0; next
        }
        /ManagedSpaceID/ { if (seen) n++ }
        END {
            if (!seen) { exit }
            counts = counts " " n
            sub(/^ /, "", counts)
            print counts
        }'
}

# The Option+1..9 bindings written above are positional slots: macOS accepts
# the write for a Space that doesn't exist, and the key then does nothing.
# That looks exactly like a broken keybinding, so say which ones are dead.
check_spaces_count() {
    if ! _per_display=$(count_spaces_per_display); then
        warn "could not read the Spaces configuration; if Option+1..9 doesn't switch desktops, create nine Spaces in Mission Control (Ctrl-Up, then +)"
        return 0
    fi
    # No display in the plist is not "no Spaces": say the count is unknown
    # rather than reporting a number that was never read.
    if test -z "$_per_display"; then
        warn "the Spaces configuration named no displays, so the Space count is unknown; if Option+1..9 doesn't switch desktops, create nine Spaces in Mission Control (Ctrl-Up, then +)"
        return 0
    fi
    info "Spaces per display: $_per_display"
    _display_number=0
    for _count in $_per_display; do
        _display_number=$((_display_number + 1))
        if test "$_count" -lt 9; then
            warn "display $_display_number has $_count Space(s), so Option+$((_count + 1))..9 will do nothing there until the rest are created in Mission Control (Ctrl-Up, then +)"
        fi
    done
}

configure_spaces() {
    # With tide, its macOS settings pin the Spaces order and turn off what
    # fights it (write_tide_macos_settings); tide uses one Space per display.
    if test "$WM" = tide; then
        todo_optional "Remove the Spaces you don't need, since tide uses one per display: open Mission Control (open -a 'Mission Control' in a terminal), point at a Space along the top, and click its x."
        return 0
    fi

    info "Pinning the Spaces order"

    # "Automatically rearrange Spaces based on most recent use" is on by
    # default, and it reorders the Spaces behind the Option+1..9 bindings
    # above -- so the shortcut that switched to a given desktop a moment ago
    # switches somewhere else now. Numbered shortcuts only mean anything with
    # a fixed order.
    try defaults write com.apple.dock mru-spaces -bool false

    # The Dock owns Mission Control and reads this at launch.
    restart_app Dock "the Spaces order to be pinned"

    # After the restart: the Dock rewrites com.apple.spaces as it goes, so
    # counting before it has settled can read the pre-restart layout.
    check_spaces_count
}

# --- Hand macOS's settings to tide ---

# tide keeps macOS's own settings that this used to write with `defaults`
# (its macOS settings page, tidemo SPEC.md §15), and applies them as its
# files change: Finder, screenshots, the Dock and Mission Control, windows,
# dialogs, typing and font smoothing. So with tide, this writes setup's
# choices to tide's shared macos.json, each run, and tide does the rest.
# A change made in Tide Settings goes to macos.local.json, which this
# never touches, and wins.
#
# What they are, and why:
# - Finder shows hidden files, the path bar and every extension, and
#   doesn't animate.
# - Screenshots go to ~/Pictures/Screenshots, with no floating thumbnail
#   holding each back (tide copies new shots to the clipboard from there).
# - The Spaces order is pinned, as tide's SPEC.md §6.4 has it.
# - Mission Control's swipes and the hot corners are off: tide has its own
#   overview (§6.9), and its three-finger swipe steps through workspaces.
# - Stage Manager, Sequoia's tiling and click-to-show-desktop are off: they
#   move windows behind tide's back.
# - Windows open and resize at once, dialogs open expanded and save to
#   disk, and typing isn't corrected.
# - Font smoothing is light: sharp rather than smeared on a non-Retina
#   display, with a little more smoothing than off.
TIDE_MACOS_SETTINGS="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/tide/macos.json"

write_tide_macos_settings() {
    info "Handing macOS's settings to tide (Tide Settings, macOS)"
    try mkdir -p "${TIDE_MACOS_SETTINGS%/*}"
    try write_file "$TIDE_MACOS_SETTINGS" <<'JSON'
{
  "finder": { "showHiddenFiles": true, "showPathBar": true, "showExtensions": true, "animations": false },
  "screenshots": { "folder": "~/Pictures/Screenshots", "thumbnail": false },
  "missionControl": { "reorderSpaces": false, "hotCorners": false, "swipes": false },
  "windows": {
    "stageManager": false, "tileByDragging": false, "tileWithOption": false,
    "clickToShowDesktop": false, "animations": false
  },
  "dialogs": { "expanded": true, "saveToICloud": false },
  "typing": {
    "autocorrect": false, "capitalize": false, "smartQuotes": false,
    "smartDashes": false, "doubleSpacePeriod": false
  },
  "text": { "smoothing": "light" }
}
JSON
    # tide saves screenshots here, and macOS falls back to the Desktop for a
    # folder that isn't there.
    try mkdir -p "$SCREENSHOT_DIR"
}

# --- Configure Amethyst tiling window manager ---

# Where Amethyst is, or empty if it isn't installed. Both locations count:
# Homebrew casks land in /Applications, and a staged bundle falls back to
# ~/Applications when /Applications isn't writable.
# Whether DIR is a usable .app rather than a directory with the right name: a
# half-extracted or half-copied bundle has neither its Info.plist nor the
# Contents/MacOS the executable lives in, and calling that "installed" is how a
# failed install becomes a permanent one.
#
# One function because there are two callers -- what counts as installed, and
# what is allowed to be published -- and they have already drifted apart once,
# which let a bundle be published that the next run then treated as missing.
#
# Contents/MacOS as a directory, not Contents/MacOS/Amethyst: the executable's
# name comes from CFBundleExecutable and isn't ours to assume. Guessing it
# wrong would make a perfectly good install look unusable, and the publish path
# deletes what it judges unusable.
is_usable_app() {
    test -f "$1/Contents/Info.plist" && test -d "$1/Contents/MacOS"
}

# --- Quiet Homebrew ---

# brew's own output -- download bars, pour lines, "already installed"
# warnings, caveats, auto-update news -- goes to one log per run, as apt's
# would on a quiet Linux run, and the run shows a line or two per step. The
# outermost of setup and setup-macos starts the log; a helper it runs adds
# to it, through the exported path. --verbose shows it instead, as - does.
start_setup_log() {
    test -z "${SETUP_LOG:-}" || return 0
    if test "${VERBOSE:-no}" = yes; then
        SETUP_LOG=-
        export SETUP_LOG
        return 0
    fi
    SETUP_LOG="${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/setup/setup.log"
    # printf, not :, which is a special builtin: dash exits when its
    # redirection fails, even under if.
    if ! mkdir -p "${SETUP_LOG%/*}" || ! printf "" > "$SETUP_LOG"; then
        warn "couldn't make $SETUP_LOG to keep the installers' output in, so it's shown instead"
        SETUP_LOG=-
    fi
    export SETUP_LOG
}

# brew_logged ARGS...: runs brew ARGS with its output added to the log. A
# failure shows what brew said this time, which is where the reason is; the
# caller says what's lost. An administrator password a .pkg cask asks for
# still reaches the terminal: sudo asks on it, not on brew's output.
brew_logged() {
    start_setup_log
    if test "$SETUP_LOG" = -; then
        brew "$@"
        return
    fi
    _bl_start=$(($(wc -l < "$SETUP_LOG") + 1))
    printf '+ brew %s\n' "$*" >> "$SETUP_LOG"
    brew "$@" >> "$SETUP_LOG" 2>&1 && return 0
    _bl_status=$?
    printf "'brew %s' exited with status %s; the end of what it said (all of it is in %s):\n" \
        "$*" "$_bl_status" "$SETUP_LOG" >&2
    tail -n "+$((_bl_start + 1))" "$SETUP_LOG" | tail -n 5 | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
    return "$_bl_status"
}

# Whether Homebrew still lists cask $1 though its app, $2, is missing. If so,
# this warns with the command that clears the record, followed by $3: what stays
# broken until then, or what the caller tries next.
#
# Something removed the app without telling brew: its own uninstaller, or a
# deleted app. brew install can't repair that. It skips a cask it thinks is
# current, and upgrading an older one first runs that version's uninstall
# steps, which can fail on what's gone. Karabiner's uninstall script went with
# the rest of it, so its run failed after the download with "uninstall script
# ... does not exist". --force skips what's missing. Removing the record is the
# user's call (Karabiner's asks for a password), so say how rather than do it.
# brew list's "not installed" error is the expected answer, so it's dropped; a
# brew that can't answer at all reads as no record, and the install goes ahead.
stale_cask_record() {
    if ! _listed=$(brew list --cask --versions "$1" 2>/dev/null); then
        return 1
    fi
    warn "Homebrew lists $_listed as installed but $2 is missing, so brew can't reinstall it; run 'brew uninstall --cask --force $1' and rerun setup-macos; $3"
}

amethyst_path() {
    for _dir in "$APPLICATIONS_DIR" "$USER_APPLICATIONS_DIR"; do
        if is_usable_app "$_dir/Amethyst.app"; then
            printf '%s' "$_dir/Amethyst.app"
            return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

# Unpack a staged Amethyst zip, the offline path. Mirrors setup-kde's staged
# Krohnkite bundle: same three places, same reason -- a machine with no network
# still has to come up configured.
#
# `brew fetch --cask amethyst` produces exactly this file on a connected
# machine, so staging one is a copy rather than a build.
install_amethyst_from_bundle() {
    _bundle=""
    for _cand in "${AMETHYST_BUNDLE:-}" \
            "$SCRIPT_DIR/amethyst.zip" \
            "$HOME/amethyst.zip"; do
        if test -n "$_cand" && test -f "$_cand"; then
            _bundle="$_cand"
            break
        fi
    done
    if test -z "$_bundle"; then
        warn "no Amethyst bundle staged (fetch one on a connected machine with 'brew fetch --cask amethyst' and copy it to $SCRIPT_DIR/amethyst.zip); the tiling settings below will have no effect until Amethyst is installed"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! is_command unzip; then
        warn "unzip not found; cannot unpack $_bundle"
        return 0
    fi

    # /Applications needs admin rights this script doesn't ask for, so fall
    # back to ~/Applications, which Amethyst runs from just as well.
    _dest="$APPLICATIONS_DIR"
    if ! test -w "$_dest"; then
        _dest="$USER_APPLICATIONS_DIR"
        info "$APPLICATIONS_DIR is not writable; installing Amethyst to $_dest"
        try mkdir -p "$_dest"
    fi

    info "Installing Amethyst from bundle: $_bundle"

    # Extracted beside the destination and moved into place only once it's
    # whole. unzip writes as it goes, so extracting straight into $_dest would
    # leave a partial Amethyst.app behind on a truncated archive or a full
    # disk -- and amethyst_path would then read that as installed and skip
    # both install paths on every later run, so a transient failure would
    # stick permanently. Same reason write_file stages its plists.
    #
    # Staging inside $_dest keeps the move a rename within one filesystem
    # rather than a second copy that can fail halfway itself.
    _staging="$_dest/.amethyst-install.$$"
    # Checked, unlike the cleanups below: those run after a failure that's
    # already being reported, but this one guards what gets published. A
    # leftover staging directory from a crashed run with this PID would be
    # extracted over rather than replaced, and the stale files would ship.
    if ! rm -rf "$_staging"; then
        warn "could not clear $_staging"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! mkdir -p "$_staging"; then
        warn "could not create $_staging"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! unzip -oq "$_bundle" -d "$_staging"; then
        # The cleanup's own status is deliberately not reported here or in the
        # paths below: the failure being cleaned up after is already going to
        # warn, and a second warning about the tidy-up would bury it.
        rm -rf "$_staging"
        warn "could not unpack $_bundle"
        return 0
    fi
    # The same invariant amethyst_path applies, via the same function, so the
    # two can't disagree about what counts as an app.
    if ! is_usable_app "$_staging/Amethyst.app"; then
        rm -rf "$_staging"
        warn "$_bundle did not contain a usable Amethyst.app"
        return 0
    fi

    # Gatekeeper refuses to launch anything carrying com.apple.quarantine, and
    # a hand-unpacked zip always does. Clearing it here means this path does
    # NOT get the signature check `brew install --cask` performs -- it trusts
    # the bundle because a person deliberately staged it. Prefer the brew path
    # where there's a network.
    #
    # Done in staging, so what lands in $_dest is already launchable.
    if is_command xattr; then
        xattr -d -r com.apple.quarantine "$_staging/Amethyst.app" 2>/dev/null
        # xattr exits non-zero both when the attribute was already absent and
        # when it couldn't be removed, so its status can't tell those apart --
        # the same trap as reading killall's. Ask what is actually true
        # instead, and don't publish an app Gatekeeper will refuse.
        if xattr -r -p com.apple.quarantine "$_staging/Amethyst.app" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            rm -rf "$_staging"
            warn "could not clear the quarantine attribute on $_bundle; macOS would refuse to launch it, so it was not installed"
            return 0
        fi
    else
        warn "xattr not found; if macOS refuses to open Amethyst, clear its quarantine attribute by hand"
    fi

    # An unusable Amethyst.app can be sitting in the destination: amethyst_path
    # treats one with no Info.plist as not installed, which is how this got
    # here. `mv src dir` moves the source *inside* an existing directory and
    # returns 0 -- the trap write_file already guards against -- which would
    # bury the staged copy at Amethyst.app/Amethyst.app, leave the broken outer
    # bundle in place, and report success.
    if test -e "$_dest/Amethyst.app"; then
        info "Replacing the unusable $_dest/Amethyst.app"
        if ! rm -rf "$_dest/Amethyst.app"; then
            rm -rf "$_staging"
            warn "could not remove the unusable $_dest/Amethyst.app"
            return 0
        fi
    fi

    if ! mv "$_staging/Amethyst.app" "$_dest/Amethyst.app"; then
        rm -rf "$_staging"
        warn "could not move Amethyst.app into $_dest"
        return 0
    fi
    rm -rf "$_staging"
}

install_amethyst() {
    if amethyst_path >/dev/null; then
        return 0
    fi
    if test "$INSTALL" = no; then
        warn "Amethyst is not installed and --no-install was given; the tiling settings below will have no effect until it is"
        return 0
    fi

    if ! is_command brew; then
        info "Homebrew not found; looking for a staged Amethyst bundle"
    elif ! stale_cask_record amethyst Amethyst "trying a staged bundle meanwhile"; then
        info "Installing Amethyst"
        if brew_logged install --cask amethyst; then
            return 0
        fi
        warn "could not install Amethyst via Homebrew; trying a staged bundle"
    fi
    install_amethyst_from_bundle
}

configure_amethyst() {
    info "Configuring Amethyst"

    # Configuring an application that isn't there writes preferences nothing
    # will ever read: `defaults` creates the domain regardless, so without this
    # the run reported success over a machine with no tiling at all.
    install_amethyst

    # Amethyst config lives in ~/conf/amethyst.yml and is symlinked
    # to ~/.amethyst.yml by confinst. Nothing to do here.
    if ! test -f "$HOME/.amethyst.yml"; then
        warn "$HOME/.amethyst.yml not found; run confinst after cloning the conf repo"
    fi

    # Disable focus follows mouse. The KDE side runs FocusFollowsMouse, but
    # KWin exempts popups from it -- see the FocusUnderMouse note in setup-kde,
    # where the same problem broke the application launcher. Amethyst has no
    # such exemption: it hit-tests only the windows it tracks, so an untracked
    # overlay (a Chrome extension popover, a System Settings panel) is invisible
    # to it and it focuses whatever sits underneath, dismissing the overlay the
    # moment the pointer reaches it. Upstream has had this open since 2015 as
    # ianyh/Amethyst#277.
    #
    # Written as false rather than left unset: a machine configured by an
    # earlier run already has true in its preferences, and dropping the line
    # would leave that stale value in place.
    info "Disabling focus follows mouse (dismisses popovers; ianyh/Amethyst#277)"
    try defaults write com.amethyst.Amethyst focus-follows-mouse -bool false

    # Amethyst can't move a window without Accessibility access, and macOS only
    # grants that through an interactive prompt -- there's no defaults key for
    # it, so say so rather than leave it looking configured but inert.
    todo_accessibility Amethyst "It can't tile without it."
}

# --- Configure tide (Hammerspoon) ---

# Where NAME.app is, or empty if it isn't installed, by the same rule as
# amethyst_path: either Applications directory, and only a usable bundle.
app_path() {
    for _dir in "$APPLICATIONS_DIR" "$USER_APPLICATIONS_DIR"; do
        if is_usable_app "$_dir/$1.app"; then
            printf '%s' "$_dir/$1.app"
            return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

hammerspoon_path() {
    app_path Hammerspoon
}

# What a machine without Hammerspoon or tide loses, for the warnings below.
TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT="nothing will tile and tide's workspace keys will do nothing"

# Homebrew only. Unlike Amethyst there's no staged-bundle path yet: tide
# itself comes from a git clone, so a machine with no network can't get it
# either way. The cask installs into /Applications without an administrator
# prompt, so --no-sudo doesn't skip it.
install_hammerspoon() {
    if hammerspoon_path >/dev/null; then
        return 0
    fi
    if test "$INSTALL" = no; then
        warn "Hammerspoon is not installed and --no-install was given; $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! is_command brew; then
        warn "Homebrew not found, so Hammerspoon can't be installed; $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    if stale_cask_record hammerspoon Hammerspoon "until then, $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT"; then
        return 0
    fi
    info "Installing Hammerspoon"
    brew_logged install --cask hammerspoon \
        || warn "could not install Hammerspoon; $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
}

# What a tide machine without blueutil loses: tide reads whether
# Bluetooth is on through it (tide's SPEC.md, section 8), and hides that
# icon without it. Its headphones icons need nothing from here.
BLUEUTIL_MISSING_EFFECT="tide's bar won't show whether Bluetooth is on"

# What a tide machine without its DDC tool loses: a monitor's own speakers
# have no volume macOS can read, so tide asks the monitor over DDC/CI
# (tide's SPEC.md, section 8), and without the tool the speaker shows no
# level.
DDC_MISSING_EFFECT="tide's bar won't show a monitor's speaker volume"

# The DDC tool for this Mac: m1ddc on Apple silicon, ddcctl on Intel, whose
# IOFramebuffer calls don't reach displays on Apple silicon.
ddc_tool() {
    if test "$(uname -m)" = arm64; then
        echo m1ddc
    else
        echo ddcctl
    fi
}

# Installs Homebrew formula $1, a command tide runs, saying what's lost
# ($2) while it's missing. A formula (free, open source), not a cask, so it
# needs no administrator and --no-sudo doesn't skip it. It's a command, so
# it's found on PATH rather than in an Applications directory.
install_brew_tool() {
    _tool=$1
    _effect=$2
    if is_command "$_tool"; then
        return 0
    fi
    if test "$INSTALL" = no; then
        warn "$_tool is not installed and --no-install was given; $_effect until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! is_command brew; then
        warn "Homebrew not found, so $_tool can't be installed; $_effect until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    info "Installing $_tool"
    brew_logged install "$_tool" \
        || warn "could not install $_tool; $_effect until it is"
}

# Clone tide if there's no checkout, then copy its modules into
# ~/.hammerspoon/tide with its own `make install`, so what lands there is
# whatever its Makefile says belongs there rather than a list kept here.
#
# An existing checkout is pulled with --ff-only first, as setup pulls tide, so
# a plain run keeps it current: it's config, not a package, so it isn't held
# to setup's weekly upgrades. A checkout with commits of its own, or
# changes a pull would overwrite, is left as it is, with a warning, and
# installed as it stands. --no-install skips the clone and the pull, which
# would bring in a newer tide -- copying an existing checkout's files is
# configuring what's already there. That includes one from before the rename,
# whose `make install` writes ~/.hammerspoon/timo instead; the init.lua written
# below loads tide, so that's checked for afterwards and a stale checkout
# gets told to update.
# default_branch DIR: origin's default branch, as DIR's clone recorded it,
# or main or master when it recorded none.
default_branch() {
    if _db=$(git -C "$1" symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD 2>/dev/null); then
        printf '%s\n' "${_db#origin/}"
        return 0
    fi
    for _db in main master; do
        if git -C "$1" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/remotes/origin/$_db"; then
            printf '%s\n' "$_db"
            return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

# pull_checkout DIR USE GIT...: runs GIT..., a git pull of DIR. When it
# fails, or leaves DIR on a branch other than origin's default, it warns why
# and what happens instead (USE): nothing here switches a branch or drops a
# change. Fails when the pull did.
pull_checkout() {
    _pc_dir=$1
    _pc_use=$2
    _pc_said=
    shift 2
    # The branch is read before pulling: a rebase that stops on conflicts
    # leaves HEAD detached. symbolic-ref exits 1 for a detached HEAD, and
    # more when it can't say.
    _pc_sym=0
    _pc_branch=$(git -C "$_pc_dir" symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD 2>/dev/null) || _pc_sym=$?
    _pc_status=0
    _pc_out=$("$@" 2>&1) || _pc_status=$?
    if test "$_pc_status" -eq 0; then
        test -z "$_pc_out" || printf '%s\n' "$_pc_out"
        if test -n "$_pc_branch" && _pc_default=$(default_branch "$_pc_dir") \
                && test -n "$_pc_default" && test "$_pc_branch" != "$_pc_default"; then
            warn "$_pc_dir is on branch $_pc_branch, not $_pc_default; $_pc_use"
        fi
        return 0
    fi
    case "$_pc_out" in
        *CONFLICT* | *"merge failed"*) _pc_sym=conflicts ;;
    esac
    if test "$_pc_sym" = conflicts; then
        _pc_why="it has conflicts to resolve"
    elif test "$_pc_sym" -eq 1; then
        _pc_why="it's detached at $(git -C "$_pc_dir" rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)"
    elif test "$_pc_sym" -eq 0 && ! git -C "$_pc_dir" rev-parse --abbrev-ref '@{u}' >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        _pc_why="branch $_pc_branch has no upstream"
    else
        # Only what says the network failed: a server that answers and
        # refuses (an HTTP 403, a rejected key) gets git's reason, below.
        case "$_pc_out" in
            *"Could not resolve host"* | *"Failed to connect to"* | *"Couldn't connect to server"* \
            | *"timed out"* | *"Network is unreachable"* | *"Connection refused"*)
                _pc_why="can't reach origin" ;;
            *"would be overwritten"* | *"local changes"* | *"unstaged changes"* \
            | *"uncommitted changes"* | *"commit your changes"* | *"commit or stash"*)
                _pc_why="local changes are in the way" ;;
            *"fast-forward"* | *"divergent"* | *"diverged"*)
                _pc_why="it has diverged from $(git -C "$_pc_dir" rev-parse --abbrev-ref '@{u}' 2>/dev/null)" ;;
            *)
                # git's own reason, with any credentials in a URL taken out,
                # and all of what git said under it when that's more than the
                # one line: the reason can come before git's line (ssh's
                # "Permission denied (publickey)") or instead of it (a hook's
                # refusal).
                _pc_said=$(printf '%s\n' "$_pc_out" | sed 's#://[^/@ ]*@#://***@#g')
                _pc_why=$(printf '%s\n' "$_pc_said" | awk '/^(fatal|error): / { sub(/^(fatal|error): /, ""); print; exit }')
                if test -z "$_pc_why"; then
                    _pc_why="git pull exited $_pc_status"
                elif test "$(printf '%s\n' "$_pc_said" | wc -l)" -eq 1; then
                    _pc_said=
                fi ;;
        esac
    fi
    warn "couldn't update $_pc_dir: $_pc_why; $_pc_use"
    test -z "$_pc_said" || printf '%s\n' "$_pc_said" | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
    return "$_pc_status"
}

install_tide() {
    case "$TIDEMO_DIR" in
        "$HOME/tilemo" | "$HOME/timo")
            info "Using the checkout in $TIDEMO_DIR, cloned before its repo was renamed tidemo; rename it to $HOME/tidemo whenever suits"
            ;;
    esac
    if test -d "$TIDEMO_DIR"; then
        if test "$INSTALL" = no; then
            info "Installing $TIDEMO_DIR as it stands, without pulling it (--no-install)"
        elif ! is_command git; then
            warn "git not found, so $TIDEMO_DIR isn't updated; installing it as it stands"
        else
            pull_checkout "$TIDEMO_DIR" "installing it as it is" git -C "$TIDEMO_DIR" pull --ff-only || :
        fi
    else
        if test "$INSTALL" = no; then
            warn "$TIDEMO_DIR not found and --no-install was given; clone $TIDEMO_REPO there and re-run, or $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT"
            return 0
        fi
        if ! is_command git; then
            warn "git not found, so tide can't be cloned into $TIDEMO_DIR; $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
            return 0
        fi
        info "Cloning tide into $TIDEMO_DIR"
        # git removes the directory it created when a clone fails, so a failure
        # here leaves nothing for the next run to mistake for a checkout.
        if ! git clone "$TIDEMO_REPO" "$TIDEMO_DIR"; then
            warn "could not clone $TIDEMO_REPO into $TIDEMO_DIR; $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
            return 0
        fi
    fi
    if ! is_command make; then
        warn "make not found, so tide can't be installed from $TIDEMO_DIR; $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    info "Installing tide from $TIDEMO_DIR"
    if ! make -C "$TIDEMO_DIR" install; then
        warn "could not install tide from $TIDEMO_DIR; $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! test -d "$HOME/.hammerspoon/tide"; then
        warn "$TIDEMO_DIR installed no $HOME/.hammerspoon/tide, so it predates the rename from tilemo; update it (git -C $TIDEMO_DIR pull) and re-run, or require(\"tide\") will fail"
    fi
}

# Hammerspoon runs ~/.hammerspoon/init.lua and nothing else, so tide only
# starts if that file loads it. Written only when there's no file at all: an
# existing init.lua is someone's own Hammerspoon config, and overwriting it
# would lose it. One that doesn't load tide gets the lines to add instead.
configure_hammerspoon_init() {
    _hs_dir="$HOME/.hammerspoon"
    _hs_init="$_hs_dir/init.lua"
    # -L as well as -e, so a dangling symlink (say, into a conf checkout that
    # moved) counts as someone's file rather than being replaced by ours.
    if test -e "$_hs_init" || test -L "$_hs_init"; then
        # require("tide"), require 'tide' and require "tide" all load it,
        # as do require("tilemo") and require("timo"), its old names, which
        # tide keeps as shims; one after a -- is commented out and doesn't.
        if grep -qE "^[^-]*require[[:space:]]*[(]?[[:space:]]*[\"'](tide|tilemo|timo)[\"']" "$_hs_init"; then
            _hs_status=0
        else
            _hs_status=$?
        fi
        case "$_hs_status" in
            0)
                detail "Skipping $_hs_init: it already loads tide"
                ;;
            1)
                warn "$_hs_init exists and doesn't load tide, so it was left alone; add these two lines to it, or $TIDEMO_MISSING_EFFECT: tide = require(\"tide\") and tide.start({})"
                ;;
            *)
                warn "could not read $_hs_init (grep exited $_hs_status); make sure it loads tide with tide = require(\"tide\") and tide.start({})"
                ;;
        esac
        return 0
    fi
    info "Writing $_hs_init to start tide"
    try mkdir -p "$_hs_dir"
    try write_file "$_hs_init" <<'LUA'
tide = require("tide")
tide.start({})
LUA
}

# Warn about another tiler, NAME, being installed or running: two tilers
# fight over the same windows, each undoing the other's layout. Warned rather
# than removed -- uninstalling an app is a bigger step than this run should
# take on its own, and switching back to Amethyst is a re-run away.
warn_if_other_tiler() {
    _tiler="$1"
    _tiler_why="$2"
    if _tiler_path=$(app_path "$_tiler"); then
        warn "$_tiler is installed at $_tiler_path; quit it and remove it from System Settings > General > Login Items, since $_tiler_why"
        return 0
    fi
    # Not installed where this looks, but it could still be running from
    # somewhere else.
    is_command pgrep || return 0
    if app_running "$_tiler"; then
        _tiler_running=0
    else
        _tiler_running=$?
    fi
    case "$_tiler_running" in
        0)
            warn "$_tiler is running; quit it and remove it from System Settings > General > Login Items, since $_tiler_why"
            ;;
        1) ;;
        *)
            warn "could not tell whether $_tiler is running (pgrep exited $_tiler_running); quit it if it is, since $_tiler_why"
            ;;
    esac
}

configure_tide() {
    info "Configuring tide"

    install_hammerspoon
    install_brew_tool blueutil "$BLUEUTIL_MISSING_EFFECT"
    install_brew_tool "$(ddc_tool)" "$DDC_MISSING_EFFECT"
    install_tide
    configure_hammerspoon_init

    warn_if_other_tiler Amethyst "it and tide would both tile the same windows"
    # Rectangle's default shortcuts sit on Ctrl+Option+arrows (tide's
    # SPEC.md, section 7.3, unverified there), the chord tide takes.
    warn_if_other_tiler Rectangle "its default shortcuts sit on Ctrl+Option+arrows, which tide needs"

    # tide draws its own bar along the top, over the menu bar (tide's
    # SPEC.md, section 8). An auto-hidden menu bar slides in over it whenever
    # the pointer reaches the top edge, so keep it shown; the Apple logo's
    # Show Menu Bar reveals it. Apps read it at launch, so it takes effect
    # everywhere after the next login.
    try defaults write NSGlobalDomain _HIHideMenuBar -bool false
    info "The menu bar stays shown, under tide's bar, after the next login"
    needs_logout "the menu bar setting"

    # Same as Amethyst's: no defaults key grants it, so say so rather than
    # leave tide looking configured but inert.
    todo_accessibility Hammerspoon "tide runs in it, and can't tile without it." \
        "Then click Hammerspoon's icon in the menu bar and choose Reload Config, so tide starts again with it."
}

# --- Configure Karabiner-Elements ---

# Where Karabiner reads add-on rules from. confinst creates the directory for
# real and symlinks the rule into it, so this is a symlink into the conf repo
# rather than a file Karabiner owns -- Karabiner rewrites karabiner.json on
# every UI change but never touches an asset.
KARABINER_RULE="$HOME/.config/karabiner/assets/complex_modifications/pc-alt-tab.json"
# That rule's name since conf gave Alt+Tab to tide. A checkout from before
# names it "Alt+Tab switches applications" and swaps Ctrl+Tab and Alt+Tab both
# ways, so the name tells the two apart.
KARABINER_TAB="Ctrl+Tab goes to the next tab"
# What a machine without working Karabiner loses, for the install warnings.
KARABINER_MISSING_EFFECT="Ctrl+Tab will switch applications rather than tabs, Ctrl+Left/Right will jump to the line ends, Win+Left/Right will move by word rather than switching Spaces, Win+Shift+Left/Right will select by word rather than moving the window a Space, Ctrl in kitty will be Command rather than Control, and Ctrl+U won't clear the password at the lock screen"
# The second rule, alongside it: Ctrl+arrow word movement, Win+arrow Space
# switching and Win+Shift+arrow moving the window a Space. Checked separately, since a conf checkout older than the rule has
# the first without the second.
KARABINER_ARROW_RULE="$HOME/.config/karabiner/assets/complex_modifications/pc-arrows.json"
# The same file's second rule, which makes Home and End go to the line's
# start and end outside terminals, as on a PC; a Mac's scroll the document.
KARABINER_HOME_END="Home and End go to the line's start and end"
# The part of that rule's name that a checkout from before Win+Shift+arrow
# lacks, so a stale rule can be told apart from a current one.
KARABINER_THROW="Win+Shift+Left/Right moves the window a Space"
# The rule that gives kitty Ctrl as Control and Alt as Meta, so Ctrl+U and
# the like reach the shell. Checked separately, for older conf checkouts.
KARABINER_TERMINAL_RULE="$HOME/.config/karabiner/assets/complex_modifications/pc-terminal.json"
# The same file's second rule, which puts the interrupt (Control+C) on
# physical Ctrl+Shift+C in Terminal and iTerm2, whose copy is Ctrl+C.
KARABINER_INTERRUPT="Ctrl+Shift+C interrupts in Terminal and iTerm2"
# The rule that makes the physical Win key tide's Super (Ctrl+Option+Command),
# for --wm tide. Its name starts "tide:".
KARABINER_TIDE_RULE="$HOME/.config/karabiner/assets/complex_modifications/tide.json"
# The same rule from conf checkouts older than the renames, named "tilemo:"
# and "timo:". tide accepts those prefixes too, so they still work.
KARABINER_TILEMO_RULE="$HOME/.config/karabiner/assets/complex_modifications/tilemo.json"
KARABINER_TIMO_RULE="$HOME/.config/karabiner/assets/complex_modifications/timo.json"
# The rule that makes Ctrl+U clear the password at the lock screen, as on
# Linux. Checked separately, for older conf checkouts.
KARABINER_LOCK_RULE="$HOME/.config/karabiner/assets/complex_modifications/pc-lock.json"

# Where Karabiner is, or empty if it isn't installed. Same is_usable_app test
# as amethyst_path, and for the same reason: an interrupted install leaves a
# directory with the right name and nothing in it, and treating that as
# installed turns a failed install into a permanent one -- every later run
# skips Homebrew and then tells the user to enable a rule in an application
# that can't start.
karabiner_path() {
    for _dir in "$APPLICATIONS_DIR" "$USER_APPLICATIONS_DIR"; do
        if is_usable_app "$_dir/Karabiner-Elements.app"; then
            printf '%s' "$_dir/Karabiner-Elements.app"
            return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

# The modifier rotation makes the physical Ctrl key send Command, which is what
# keeps Ctrl+C and friends where a Linux hand expects them. Tab is the one
# binding that inverts under it -- macOS switches applications with Cmd+Tab
# where Linux uses Alt+Tab -- so app switching lands on the physical Ctrl key.
# The rule in the conf repo puts the next tab back there; physical Alt+Tab is
# tide's, in tide's rule, and the next tab without it. See
# config/karabiner/README.md there for why the rule emits left_option.
#
# Homebrew only. Unlike Amethyst there's no staged-bundle path: Karabiner
# installs a driver extension that needs approving in the GUI anyway, so an
# offline install would still leave the interactive half undone.
install_karabiner() {
    if karabiner_path >/dev/null; then
        return 0
    fi
    if test "$INSTALL" = no; then
        warn "Karabiner-Elements is not installed and --no-install was given; $KARABINER_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    # Not attempted under --no-sudo: the cask is a .pkg, so Homebrew hands it
    # to the system installer and an administrator prompt appears -- exactly
    # what --no-sudo exists to avoid.
    if test "$SUDO" = no; then
        warn "Karabiner-Elements' installer needs administrator rights and --no-sudo was given; skipping it, so $KARABINER_MISSING_EFFECT"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! is_command brew; then
        warn "Homebrew not found, so Karabiner-Elements can't be installed; $KARABINER_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    if stale_cask_record karabiner-elements Karabiner-Elements "until then, $KARABINER_MISSING_EFFECT"; then
        return 0
    fi
    info "Installing Karabiner-Elements; its installer may ask for your password"
    brew_logged install --cask karabiner-elements \
        || warn "could not install Karabiner-Elements; $KARABINER_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
}

# The title Karabiner lists rule file $1 under, from its "title", or nothing.
karabiner_title() {
    sed -n 's/^[[:space:]]*"title"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' "$1" 2>/dev/null | sed -n 1p
}

# Add a rule to enable, to _karabiner_rules: one whose name starts $1, in
# rule file $2, listed under that file's title when it has one, as
# Karabiner's "Add predefined rule" list shows it.
karabiner_rule() {
    _kr_title=$(karabiner_title "$2")
    if test -n "$_kr_title"; then
        _kr_line="- under \"$_kr_title\", the rule starting \"$1\""
    else
        _kr_line="- the rule starting \"$1\""
    fi
    _karabiner_rules="${_karabiner_rules:+$_karabiner_rules
}$_kr_line"
}

configure_karabiner() {
    info "Configuring Karabiner-Elements"

    install_karabiner

    # The rules to enable, a line each, in the order to enable them, and
    # whether conf's rules need updating first.
    _karabiner_rules=""
    _karabiner_stale=no
    if test -f "$KARABINER_RULE" && grep -qF "$KARABINER_TAB" "$KARABINER_RULE"; then
        karabiner_rule "$KARABINER_TAB" "$KARABINER_RULE"
    elif test -f "$KARABINER_RULE" && test "$WM" = tide; then
        # The old rule makes Alt+Tab Cmd+Tab, which is tide's key now, so
        # it isn't named; updating the checkout brings the current one.
        warn "$KARABINER_RULE predates tide taking Alt+Tab; update the conf repo and run confinst, then enable \"$KARABINER_TAB\" in place of \"Alt+Tab switches applications\", or Ctrl+Tab keeps switching applications"
        _karabiner_stale=yes
    elif test -f "$KARABINER_RULE"; then
        # Without tide the old rule still works as it always did, so it's
        # named as it is, as the stale arrow rule is below.
        info "$KARABINER_RULE predates tide taking Alt+Tab and still makes Alt+Tab switch applications; update the conf repo and run confinst for the current rule"
        karabiner_rule "Alt+Tab switches applications" "$KARABINER_RULE"
    else
        warn "$KARABINER_RULE not found; run confinst after cloning the conf repo, or Ctrl+Tab keeps switching applications"
        _karabiner_stale=yes
    fi
    if test -f "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE" && grep -qF "$KARABINER_THROW" "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE"; then
        karabiner_rule "Ctrl+Left/Right moves by word" "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE"
        # It matches nothing the others do, so it can go anywhere.
        if grep -qF "$KARABINER_HOME_END" "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE"; then
            karabiner_rule "$KARABINER_HOME_END" "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE"
        else
            info "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE predates \"$KARABINER_HOME_END\"; update the conf repo for it, or Home and End keep scrolling the document"
        fi
    elif test -f "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE"; then
        # A conf checkout from before the Win+Shift row: re-adding that rule
        # can't bring the binding, so updating the checkout comes first.
        warn "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE predates Win+Shift+Left/Right; update the conf repo, then remove and add the arrow rule again, or Win+Shift+Left/Right won't move the window a Space"
        karabiner_rule "Ctrl+Left/Right moves by word" "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE"
        _karabiner_stale=yes
    else
        warn "$KARABINER_ARROW_RULE not found; update the conf repo and run confinst, or Win+Left/Right won't switch Spaces, Win+Shift+Left/Right won't move the window a Space, and Ctrl+Left/Right keeps jumping to the line ends"
        _karabiner_stale=yes
    fi
    # The rules below go above the arrow rule, because Karabiner applies the
    # first rule that matches a key: the kitty rule and the arrow rule both
    # want Ctrl+Left/Right, and the tide rule and the arrow rule both want
    # the physical Win key. Karabiner puts each rule enabled at the top, so
    # enabling them after it does that.
    if test -f "$KARABINER_TERMINAL_RULE"; then
        karabiner_rule "Ctrl is Control and Alt is Meta in kitty" "$KARABINER_TERMINAL_RULE"
        # It matches nothing the others do, so it can go anywhere.
        if grep -qF "$KARABINER_INTERRUPT" "$KARABINER_TERMINAL_RULE"; then
            karabiner_rule "$KARABINER_INTERRUPT" "$KARABINER_TERMINAL_RULE"
        else
            info "$KARABINER_TERMINAL_RULE predates \"$KARABINER_INTERRUPT\"; update the conf repo for it, or Terminal and iTerm2 keep the interrupt on Alt+C"
        fi
    else
        warn "$KARABINER_TERMINAL_RULE not found; update the conf repo and run confinst, or Ctrl in kitty stays Command and Ctrl+U, Ctrl+K and the like won't reach the shell"
        _karabiner_stale=yes
    fi
    if test "$WM" = tide; then
        if test -f "$KARABINER_TIDE_RULE"; then
            karabiner_rule "tide:" "$KARABINER_TIDE_RULE"
        elif test -f "$KARABINER_TILEMO_RULE"; then
            info "$KARABINER_TILEMO_RULE predates the rename to tide and still works; update the conf repo and run confinst to get the \"tide:\" rule"
            karabiner_rule "tilemo:" "$KARABINER_TILEMO_RULE"
        elif test -f "$KARABINER_TIMO_RULE"; then
            info "$KARABINER_TIMO_RULE predates the rename to tide and still works; update the conf repo and run confinst to get the \"tide:\" rule"
            karabiner_rule "timo:" "$KARABINER_TIMO_RULE"
        else
            warn "$KARABINER_TIDE_RULE not found; run confinst after updating the conf repo, or tide falls back to Option for its Super key"
            _karabiner_stale=yes
        fi
    fi
    # Last, above every other, tide's included, which would take a Mac
    # keyboard's Command+U at the lock screen as Super+U.
    if test -f "$KARABINER_LOCK_RULE"; then
        karabiner_rule "Ctrl+U clears the password at the lock screen" "$KARABINER_LOCK_RULE"
    else
        warn "$KARABINER_LOCK_RULE not found; update the conf repo and run confinst, or Ctrl+U won't clear the password at the lock screen"
        _karabiner_stale=yes
    fi

    if test "$_karabiner_stale" = yes; then
        todo "Bring conf's Karabiner rules up to date, as the warnings above say: in your conf checkout, git pull and run confinst, then run setup-macos again, for the rules this list can't name yet."
    fi

    # Nothing to set up in an app that isn't there; the install said so.
    karabiner_path >/dev/null || return 0

    todo "Finish setting up Karabiner-Elements, or none of its rules work: open Karabiner-Elements, from Applications, and click Setup in its sidebar. Allow each item it lists."

    # tide turns conf's rules on itself, editing karabiner.json, and keeps
    # Karabiner's copies up to date with them (tide's SPEC.md, section
    # 15), so with it there's nothing to enable by hand.
    #
    # Without tide, Karabiner lists an asset but doesn't enable it, and
    # this script doesn't edit karabiner.json: the app rewrites it, so
    # editing it from here would be racing the GUI. Enabling copies the rule
    # into karabiner.json, and Karabiner runs that copy, so an updated asset
    # only takes effect once the rule is added again.
    if test -n "$_karabiner_rules" && test "$WM" != tide; then
        todo "Turn on conf's Karabiner rules, or Ctrl+Tab switches applications: in Karabiner-Elements, click Complex Modifications in the sidebar, then Add predefined rule (not Add your own rule). Click Enable beside each of these, in this order; type a title in the box at the top to find one:" \
            "$_karabiner_rules" \
            "One enabled on an earlier run doesn't pick up changes to it: click the trash icon beside it in that list, then enable it again as above."
    fi

    # Karabiner leaves pointing devices alone until told, so Win+click and
    # Win+drag carry plain Option to tide (conf's config/karabiner).
    if test "$WM" = tide; then
        todo "Let Karabiner see your mouse and trackpad, for tide's Win+click, Win+drag and Win+middle-click: in Karabiner-Elements, click Devices in the sidebar and turn on Modify events beside each one."
    fi
}

# --- Configure LinearMouse ---

# macOS can't say how far a mouse wheel notch scrolls: System Settings'
# scrolling speed only changes the acceleration, and a notch at a time
# barely moves a page. LinearMouse (free, open source) sets it per device.
# It reads the first of these that exists, and makes the last when there's
# none (its ConfigurationState.swift).
LINEARMOUSE_SUPPORT_CONFIG="$HOME/Library/Application Support/linearmouse/linearmouse.json"
LINEARMOUSE_CONFIG="$HOME/.config/linearmouse/linearmouse.json"
# Lines per notch for a mouse, in the config written below: tide's 3x
# scroll speed at three lines a notch, as tide applies it. A trackpad is
# left as macOS has it.
LINEARMOUSE_LINES=9
# A mouse's pointer: macOS's acceleration and LinearMouse's speed, gentler
# than macOS's own curve and closer to libinput's adaptive profile at tide's
# sensitivity. Tried by hand on a PC mouse; a high-DPI one may want less.
LINEARMOUSE_ACCELERATION=1.5
LINEARMOUSE_SPEED=0.35
LINEARMOUSE_MISSING_EFFECT="a mouse wheel notch will keep scrolling a line or so"

linearmouse_path() {
    app_path LinearMouse
}

# Homebrew only, like Hammerspoon: the cask is an app, so --no-sudo has
# nothing to skip.
install_linearmouse() {
    if linearmouse_path >/dev/null; then
        return 0
    fi
    if test "$INSTALL" = no; then
        warn "LinearMouse is not installed and --no-install was given; $LINEARMOUSE_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    if ! is_command brew; then
        warn "Homebrew not found, so LinearMouse can't be installed; $LINEARMOUSE_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
        return 0
    fi
    if stale_cask_record linearmouse LinearMouse "until then, $LINEARMOUSE_MISSING_EFFECT"; then
        return 0
    fi
    info "Installing LinearMouse"
    brew_logged install --cask linearmouse \
        || warn "could not install LinearMouse; $LINEARMOUSE_MISSING_EFFECT until it is"
}

# The config LinearMouse reads: the first of its two places that exists.
linearmouse_config() {
    if test -e "$LINEARMOUSE_SUPPORT_CONFIG" || test -L "$LINEARMOUSE_SUPPORT_CONFIG"; then
        printf '%s' "$LINEARMOUSE_SUPPORT_CONFIG"
    else
        printf '%s' "$LINEARMOUSE_CONFIG"
    fi
}

# Whether the file at $1 is the config LinearMouse makes the first time it
# starts, which sets nothing: its "$schema" and no schemes, whatever the
# spacing (its Configuration.swift). A LinearMouse started before setup
# leaves that, and it's no more the user's than no file at all.
linearmouse_config_is_first() {
    test -f "$1" || return 1
    # The $ is the key's own, not the shell's.
    # shellcheck disable=SC2016
    tr -d ' \t\r\n' < "$1" | grep -Eq '^\{"\$schema":"[^"]*","schemes":\[\]\}$'
}

# The config is written only where there's none, or only LinearMouse's
# first. LinearMouse rewrites the file whenever its settings change, so any
# other is the user's, and a symlink into the conf repo would be replaced or
# edited behind its back.
configure_linearmouse() {
    info "Configuring LinearMouse"

    install_linearmouse

    _lm_config=$(linearmouse_config)
    if linearmouse_config_is_first "$_lm_config"; then
        info "$_lm_config is the empty one LinearMouse made as it first started; writing $LINEARMOUSE_LINES lines per mouse wheel notch and a gentler pointer acceleration over it"
        write_linearmouse_config "$_lm_config"
    elif test -e "$_lm_config" || test -L "$_lm_config"; then
        detail "Skipping LinearMouse's settings: $_lm_config exists"
    else
        info "Writing $_lm_config: $LINEARMOUSE_LINES lines per mouse wheel notch and a gentler pointer acceleration"
        write_linearmouse_config "$_lm_config"
    fi

    _linearmouse=$(linearmouse_path) || return 0
    # Started in the background so it reads the config now. It asks for
    # Accessibility the first time, which only a person can grant.
    try open -g "$_linearmouse"
    todo "Turn on LinearMouse, or a mouse wheel notch scrolls a line or so: allow it Accessibility when it asks, or turn it on yourself under System Settings > Privacy & Security > Accessibility." \
        "Then click its icon in the menu bar and choose Start at login."
}

# Write the config to $1: every mouse's notch LINEARMOUSE_LINES lines and its
# pointer LINEARMOUSE_ACCELERATION and LINEARMOUSE_SPEED, and nothing for a
# trackpad.
write_linearmouse_config() {
    try mkdir -p "$(dirname "$1")"
    try write_file "$1" <<CONFIG
{
    "schemes": [
        {
            "if": {
                "device": {
                    "category": "mouse"
                }
            },
            "scrolling": {
                "distance": {
                    "vertical": $LINEARMOUSE_LINES
                }
            },
            "pointer": {
                "acceleration": $LINEARMOUSE_ACCELERATION,
                "speed": $LINEARMOUSE_SPEED
            }
        }
    ]
}
CONFIG
}

# --- Configure application launch shortcuts ---

# Print the path to a launcher helper, or fail if there isn't one. PATH first,
# then beside this script: during a fresh bootstrap `setup` runs setup-macos
# out of the just-cloned scripts repo, before any login shell has put it on
# PATH. Baking $HOME/scripts into the workflow instead -- which is what this
# used to do -- writes a Quick Action pointing at nothing on a checkout kept
# anywhere else.
resolve_launcher() {
    _launcher=$(command -v "$1") || _launcher=""
    if test -z "$_launcher" && test -x "$SCRIPT_DIR/$1"; then
        _launcher="$SCRIPT_DIR/$1"
    fi
    test -n "$_launcher" || return 1
    printf '%s' "$_launcher"
}

# Print PATH as a single-quoted /bin/sh word, then escaped for XML, ready to
# drop into the workflow's COMMAND_STRING. Two separate hazards: the string is
# run as a shell command, so a checkout under a path with a space would be
# split into two words (and one with a `$` or a `;` would be worse); and it's
# embedded in a plist, so an `&` or a `<` in the path makes the XML invalid.
# Quote for the shell first, then escape the result for XML -- the other order
# would escape the quotes we just added.
shell_quote_xml() {
    printf '%s' "$1" \
        | sed -e "s/'/'\\\\''/g" -e "s/^/'/" -e "s/\$/'/" \
        | sed -e 's/&/\&amp;/g' -e 's/</\&lt;/g' -e 's/>/\&gt;/g'
}

# Create an Automator Quick Action (.workflow) that runs a shell script.
# These appear in System Settings > Keyboard > Shortcuts > Services, with the
# Option+key shortcuts assigned below (physical Win key sends Option).
create_launcher_workflow() {
    name="$1"
    script=$(shell_quote_xml "$2")
    workflow_dir="$SERVICES_DIR/$name.workflow"
    try mkdir -p "$workflow_dir/Contents"

    # An unquoted heredoc, so $name expands. This used to be a fixed
    # placeholder patched by `sed -i ''`, whose empty-suffix argument is
    # BSD-only -- and unnecessary, since the value is known right here.
    #
    # NSSendTypes is present and empty on purpose: that's how a service
    # declares it takes no input, which is what makes these appear whether or
    # not anything is selected.
    try write_file "$workflow_dir/Contents/Info.plist" <<PLIST
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSServices</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSMenuItem</key>
            <dict>
                <key>default</key>
                <string>$name</string>
            </dict>
            <key>NSMessage</key>
            <string>runWorkflowAsService</string>
            <key>NSSendTypes</key>
            <array/>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
PLIST

    try write_file "$workflow_dir/Contents/document.wflow" <<WFLOW
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>AMApplicationBuild</key>
    <string>523</string>
    <key>AMApplicationVersion</key>
    <string>2.10</string>
    <key>AMDocumentVersion</key>
    <string>2</string>
    <key>actions</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>action</key>
            <dict>
                <key>AMAccepts</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>Container</key>
                    <string>List</string>
                    <key>Optional</key>
                    <true/>
                    <key>Types</key>
                    <array>
                        <string>com.apple.cocoa.string</string>
                    </array>
                </dict>
                <key>AMActionVersion</key>
                <string>2.0.3</string>
                <key>AMApplication</key>
                <array>
                    <string>Automator</string>
                </array>
                <key>AMCategory</key>
                <string>AMCategoryUtilities</string>
                <key>AMIconName</key>
                <string>Run Shell Script</string>
                <key>AMName</key>
                <string>Run Shell Script</string>
                <key>AMParameterProperties</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>COMMAND_STRING</key>
                    <dict/>
                    <key>inputMethod</key>
                    <dict/>
                    <key>shell</key>
                    <dict/>
                    <key>source</key>
                    <dict/>
                </dict>
                <key>AMProvides</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>Container</key>
                    <string>List</string>
                    <key>Types</key>
                    <array>
                        <string>com.apple.cocoa.string</string>
                    </array>
                </dict>
                <key>ActionBundlePath</key>
                <string>/System/Library/Automator/Run Shell Script.action</string>
                <key>ActionName</key>
                <string>Run Shell Script</string>
                <key>BundleIdentifier</key>
                <string>com.apple.RunShellScript</string>
                <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
                <string>2.0.3</string>
                <key>CanShowSelectedItemsWhenRun</key>
                <false/>
                <key>CanShowWhenRun</key>
                <true/>
                <key>Parameters</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>COMMAND_STRING</key>
                    <string>$script</string>
                    <key>CheckedForUserDefaultShell</key>
                    <true/>
